    "explanation": "Both of you are asking me to settle the same $5.00 no-show fee the platform charged the rider and paid to the driver. I cannot decide it, because the policy sets a confidence bar this file cannot clear.\n\nThe fee is only good if the driver was actually at the Tiong Bahru Plaza pickup and waited out the free wait (POL-NS-21). The one thing that puts the driver there is the trip record's auto-arrival marker read together with the GPS trace. The pre-pass records those as a single carrier, not two, because the marker was auto-confirmed from the GPS measurement, so the trace cannot corroborate the marker as a separate source. That carrier is impaired: the trace is sparsely sampled and partial (EV-GPS-00903, weight 0.6, issue codes LOW_SAMPLE_RATE and PARTIAL_RECORD). Under POL-GEN-02(2) my confidence is capped at 0.6. Because the rider's profile carries a fraud flag of 1, POL-GEN-22 — applied to no-shows by POL-NS-26 — requires 0.95 before an AI ruling can stand. 0.6 falls short, so under POL-GEN-26 the case goes to a human reviewer with the $5.00 held: not collected and not refunded.\n\nFor the driver: much of what you say does check out. The cancellation time and the free-wait expiry come from the trip record (EV-TRIP-00903, 1.0); the contact obligation is discharged — four in-app messages and a call, all before the 08:51 cancellation (EV-CHAT-00903, 1.0, with the call also on the trip record) — so POL-NS-23 does not void the fee; and the amount charged, $5.00, is the policy fee (EV-FARE-00903, 1.0), so no POL-NS-22 fee-amount referral arises. But none of those carries the fact the fee actually needs — that the driver was at the pickup. Your own chat messages ('I've arrived at the pickup point') repeat the marker; they are your own account, not a measurement independent of it, so they do not lift the cap.\n\nFor the rider: your refund route under POL-NS-24 is closed — the record shows the driver within the arrival deadline (arrived_by_deadline true, before 08:50) — so I do not refund the fee. But I do not uphold it either.\n\nThe open question for the reviewer: whether the driver was in fact at the pickup, on more than a single impaired driver-side trace. No clause in this case produces a referral."
